Chapter: Plug Bytes

Section: Body

 The sibling of `PlugStdio` for the plugs that take a COMPILED PAYLOAD rather
 than IR text: `elf`, `pe` and `img` receive bytes and answer bytes, so
 neither `read-file-uni` nor `plug-emit-ir-stream` can express them.

 `read-file-raw` on this target reads stdin to EOF and stops at nothing else,
 NUL bytes included; on bare metal the same builtin ends at a NUL or an EOT
 because a serial ring has no end of input. That divergence is deliberate and
 is what makes a binary payload expressible here at all.

 The `+ 4` is this target's Text layout: a four-byte length followed by the
 bytes themselves, so the payload's first byte sits four past the value's own
 address. x86-64 uses an eight-byte length and this chapter is not built for
 it.

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
   payload <- read-file-raw ""
   plug-emit-bytes ((address-of payload) + 4) (text-length payload)
  end
